chived_by":null},{"id":2666708289,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwyNjY2NzA4Mjg5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/labels/topic:3d","name":"topic:3d","color":"ffee88","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2025-10-14T19:05:02Z","updated_at":"2025-10-15T10:04:20Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in Godot 4.5 stable\n\n### System information\n\nWindows 11 - Godot 4.5 Stable - Vulkan (Forward+) - RTX 3070Ti\n\n### Issue description\n\nIf I create a QuadMesh, increase subdivs, then scale it, the origin / center vertex remains in the same position regardless of scale, which I'd expect. However, if I do the same with a basic plane from Blender, the mesh moves further away from the world origin as its scaled up. This only happens in Godot, it's not observable in any other 3D software. It happens with GLB and OBJ file types.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- Create a new game project\n- Open blender, delete everything, add a plane, edit mode > subdivide, apply all transforms and export as GLTF or OBJ\n- Disable \"Generate LODs\" in Godot importer, create a MeshInstance3D and load the resource\n- Zoom into the origin point, and set the Node3D's scale to 10, 100, 1000\n- Observe the origin point of the mesh move further away from the world origin as it's scaled up\n\n<img width=\"699\" height=\"588\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7ce79fbf-2ba8-4124-a697-6bb7d61bc5a9\" />\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[new_game_project_2025-10-14_20-04-34.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/22911245/new_game_